Z||000002||PBJ||10-18-2001||01:15 PM||||I would go with to much memory, but if you were too try use hot water, really hot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||10-18-2001||01:18 PM||||I think it will definatly have a memory unless you melted it down all the way and re-molded it completely.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-18-2001||01:30 PM||||Ed Parks told me that he uses a fella near him that uses a heating process to restore bumper covers - he heats them up and memory restores them , so I would imagine that what you propose might not work. But what the heck try it, you might be on to something after all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero-lite||10-18-2001||05:41 PM||||I believe that RIM is a "thermoset" plastic.  This means it will not reform when melted.  In fact, it may not even remain the same plastic if it melts.  Don't give up though.  I once "fixed" a Honda front pumper cover made of thermoset plastic by heating.  You have to be very patient about the process.  What I did was use a heat lamp some distance away (like about 3 feet) from the back side of the damaged area.  I placed the facia so the painted side was on a couple of towels on the garage floor.  The damage was a large dent or bulge inward. I slowly heated the plastic until I could see it barely soften- It would bounce halfway back into the damaged state when released.  At that point I turned off the heat lamp, pushed the damaged area back into the original shape and held it there (the plastic was VERY HOT at this point)and sprayed water on the back side with a spray bottle.  After about 30 sprays I dumped about a cup of water on the back (which the facia held becuase of the shape) and let it all cool while holding the damaged dent in the corrected position (about 3 minutes).  It worked.  There was some slight crazing of the paint on the other side from the heat, but it looked 95% better than the damage did.  I would be afraid to sand it much after that becuase the plastic probably did break down somewhat from the heat.  Give it a try if the dent is large and there is no tearing.||||reg||1
